/* Ibory: the Hall of History (built by history/build_history.py; edit the JSON in history/, not this file) */
window.IB_HIST={"heroes":{"Julius Caesar":{"years":"100–44 BC","from":"Roman Republic","text":"Gaius Julius Caesar was a Roman general and statesman who conquered Gaul and became dictator of Rome. He is remembered as a brilliant, calculating commander whose own accounts of his campaigns are still read."},"Alexander the Great":{"years":"356–323 BC","from":"Kingdom of Macedon","text":"Alexander III was a king of Macedon who led his army from Greece to Egypt and the edge of India. Never defeated in a major battle, he built one of the largest empires of the ancient world before dying at 32."},"Hannibal Barca":{"years":"247–c. 183 BC","from":"Carthage","text":"Hannibal was a Carthaginian general who fought Rome in the Second Punic War. He is remembered for crossing the Alps with an army and elephants and for his crushing victory at Cannae in 216 BC."},"Leonidas":{"years":"d. 480 BC","from":"Sparta","text":"Leonidas I was a king of Sparta during the Persian invasion of Greece. He is remembered for leading a small Greek force, including 300 Spartans, in a last stand against a far larger Persian army at Thermopylae."},"Charlemagne":{"years":"c. 747–814","from":"Frankish Kingdom","text":"Charlemagne was king of the Franks, united much of western Europe and was crowned emperor in Rome in 800. He is remembered as a ruler who organized his lands through laws, officials and schools, sparking a revival of learning."},"Joan of Arc":{"years":"c. 1412–1431","from":"Kingdom of France","text":"Joan of Arc was a French peasant girl who said heavenly voices told her to help drive the English from France. She led troops to lift the siege of Orléans in 1429, and was later captured, executed and made a saint."},"Richard the Lionheart":{"years":"1157–1199","from":"Kingdom of England","text":"Richard I was king of England and one of the leading commanders of the Third Crusade. He earned the name Lionheart for his bravery in battle, winning victories such as Arsuf, though he never retook Jerusalem."},"Saladin":{"years":"c. 1137–1193","from":"Ayyubid Sultanate","text":"Saladin was the sultan of Egypt and Syria who founded the Ayyubid dynasty. Known for his patience and skill, he defeated the crusaders at Hattin in 1187, recaptured Jerusalem, and later held off Richard the Lionheart."},"Genghis Khan":{"years":"c. 1162–1227","from":"Mongol Empire","text":"Genghis Khan, born Temüjin, united the Mongol tribes and was proclaimed their ruler in 1206. His fast, disciplined horse armies began the conquests that created the largest land empire in history."},"Attila":{"years":"d. 453","from":"Huns","text":"Attila was ruler of the Huns, whose empire stretched across central and eastern Europe. He led powerful invasions of both the Eastern and Western Roman Empires and was feared as one of Rome's most dangerous enemies."},"El Cid":{"years":"c. 1043–1099","from":"Kingdom of Castile","text":"Rodrigo Díaz de Vivar, known as El Cid, was a Castilian knight who served both Christian and Muslim rulers in medieval Spain. He is remembered as a formidable commander who captured Valencia and ruled it until his death."},"William Wallace":{"years":"c. 1270–1305","from":"Kingdom of Scotland","text":"William Wallace was a Scottish knight who became a leader in the war for independence from England. He is remembered for his victory at Stirling Bridge in 1297 and became a lasting national hero after his capture and execution."},"Boudica":{"years":"d. c. AD 61","from":"Iceni (Britons)","text":"Boudica was queen of the Iceni, a British tribe living under Roman rule. Around AD 60 she led a large revolt that destroyed several Roman towns, including London, before her army was finally defeated."},"Cleopatra":{"years":"69–30 BC","from":"Ptolemaic Egypt","text":"Cleopatra VII was the last active ruler of Ptolemaic Egypt. Highly educated and politically skilled, she sought to protect her kingdom through alliances with Julius Caesar and Mark Antony."},"Sun Tzu":{"years":"c. 6th–5th century BC","from":"Ancient China","text":"Sun Tzu is the traditional author of The Art of War, an ancient Chinese book on strategy. Little is known for certain about his life, but the book's lessons on planning, deception and knowing your enemy are still studied today."},"Scipio Africanus":{"years":"236–183 BC","from":"Roman Republic","text":"Publius Cornelius Scipio was a Roman general of the Second Punic War. He is remembered as a clever strategist who took the war to Spain and Africa and defeated Hannibal at Zama in 202 BC."},"Xerxes":{"years":"c. 518–465 BC","from":"Achaemenid Persian Empire","text":"Xerxes I was king of the Persian Empire and son of Darius the Great. He ruled a vast realm, led a huge invasion of Greece in 480 BC, and completed grand building works at Persepolis."},"Cyrus the Great":{"years":"c. 600–530 BC","from":"Achaemenid Persian Empire","text":"Cyrus II founded the Persian Empire, conquering the Medes, Lydia and Babylon. He is remembered for ruling his many peoples with tolerance, including allowing exiled communities such as the Jews to return home."},"Ramesses II":{"years":"c. 1303–1213 BC","from":"New Kingdom Egypt","text":"Ramesses II was a pharaoh of Egypt who ruled for about 66 years. He is remembered for vast building projects such as Abu Simbel and for one of the earliest known peace treaties, made with the Hittites."},"Pericles":{"years":"c. 495–429 BC","from":"Athens","text":"Pericles was the leading statesman of Athens during its golden age. A powerful speaker, he championed Athenian democracy and the building of the Parthenon on the Acropolis."},"Themistocles":{"years":"c. 524–459 BC","from":"Athens","text":"Themistocles was an Athenian politician and general. He persuaded Athens to build a large navy, then lured the Persian fleet into the narrow straits at Salamis in 480 BC, where the Greeks won a decisive victory."},"Constantine":{"years":"c. 272–337","from":"Roman Empire","text":"Constantine the Great was a Roman emperor who reunited the empire under his rule. He is remembered for supporting Christianity and founding Constantinople, which served as an imperial capital for over a thousand years."},"Justinian":{"years":"c. 482–565","from":"Byzantine Empire","text":"Justinian I was emperor of the Eastern Roman Empire. He is remembered for gathering Roman law into one great code, building the Hagia Sophia, and reconquering lands in Italy and North Africa."},"Belisarius":{"years":"c. 500–565","from":"Byzantine Empire","text":"Belisarius was the leading general of Emperor Justinian I. Often outnumbered, he won through careful planning and tactics, retaking North Africa from the Vandals and much of Italy from the Ostrogoths."},"Charles Martel":{"years":"c. 688–741","from":"Frankish Kingdom","text":"Charles Martel was a Frankish leader who held real power as mayor of the palace. He is best known for defeating an Umayyad army near Tours in 732, and his nickname Martel means \"the Hammer\"."},"Frederick Barbarossa":{"years":"1122–1190","from":"Holy Roman Empire","text":"Frederick I, called Barbarossa for his red beard, was Holy Roman Emperor. A forceful warrior king, he campaigned repeatedly in Italy and drowned while leading an army on the Third Crusade."},"Robert the Bruce":{"years":"1274–1329","from":"Kingdom of Scotland","text":"Robert I was a king of Scotland who led the fight for independence from England. His victory at Bannockburn in 1314 was a turning point, and England recognized Scotland's independence in 1328."},"Vercingetorix":{"years":"c. 82–46 BC","from":"Gauls (Arverni)","text":"Vercingetorix was a chieftain of the Arverni who united many Gallic tribes against Julius Caesar. After early successes he was besieged at Alesia in 52 BC and surrendered, becoming a symbol of Gallic resistance."},"Spartacus":{"years":"d. 71 BC","from":"Thrace and Roman Italy","text":"Spartacus was a Thracian gladiator who escaped and led a great slave revolt against Rome. His army defeated several Roman forces across Italy before it was finally crushed in 71 BC."},"Octavian":{"years":"63 BC–AD 14","from":"Roman Empire","text":"Octavian, Caesar's adopted heir, defeated his rivals and became Rome's first emperor under the name Augustus. He is remembered as a shrewd political operator who brought peace after years of civil war and reshaped Roman government."},"Marcus Aurelius":{"years":"121–180","from":"Roman Empire","text":"Marcus Aurelius was a Roman emperor and Stoic philosopher. He spent much of his reign defending the frontiers, and his private reflections, known as the Meditations, are still widely read."},"Trajan":{"years":"53–117","from":"Roman Empire","text":"Trajan was a Roman emperor and experienced soldier. He conquered Dacia and campaigned in Mesopotamia, bringing the empire to its greatest extent, a success celebrated on Trajan's Column in Rome."},"Hadrian":{"years":"76–138","from":"Roman Empire","text":"Hadrian was a Roman emperor who travelled widely across his lands. He chose to secure the empire's borders rather than expand them, building defenses such as Hadrian's Wall in Britain."},"Tomyris":{"years":"6th century BC","from":"Massagetae","text":"Tomyris was a queen of the Massagetae, a nomadic people of Central Asia. According to the Greek historian Herodotus, her forces defeated and killed Cyrus the Great, though other ancient accounts tell his death differently."},"Zenobia":{"years":"c. 240–after 274","from":"Palmyra","text":"Zenobia was queen of Palmyra in Syria, ruling for her young son after her husband Odaenathus died. She broke away from Rome and took Egypt and much of Asia Minor before Emperor Aurelian defeated her."},"Harald Hardrada":{"years":"c. 1015–1066","from":"Kingdom of Norway","text":"Harald Hardrada became king of Norway after years as a soldier in the Varangian Guard of Constantinople. He died at Stamford Bridge in 1066 while invading England, an event often seen as the end of the Viking Age."},"William the Conqueror":{"years":"c. 1028–1087","from":"Duchy of Normandy","text":"William was the Duke of Normandy who invaded England in 1066. His victory at the Battle of Hastings made him king, and Norman rule reshaped English language, law and landscape."},"Otto the Great":{"years":"912–973","from":"Holy Roman Empire","text":"Otto I was king of the Germans and became the first Holy Roman Emperor in 962. He strengthened royal power by working closely with the Church and defeated the Magyars at Lechfeld in 955."},"Alaric":{"years":"c. 370–410","from":"Visigoths","text":"Alaric I was a king of the Visigoths. In 410 his army sacked Rome, the first time in about 800 years that the city had fallen to a foreign enemy."},"Theodoric":{"years":"c. 454–526","from":"Ostrogothic Kingdom","text":"Theodoric the Great was king of the Ostrogoths who took control of Italy in 493. He ruled from Ravenna for over thirty years, keeping Roman laws and officials and bringing a long period of peace."},"Dracula":{"years":"c. 1431–1477","from":"Principality of Wallachia","text":"Vlad III, known as Dracula, was a prince of Wallachia in what is now Romania. He is remembered for fierce resistance to the Ottoman Empire and harsh rule, and his name later inspired Bram Stoker's vampire novel."},"Sequoyah":{"years":"c. 1770–1843","from":"Cherokee Nation","text":"Sequoyah was a Cherokee silversmith who created a writing system for the Cherokee language. His syllabary, completed around 1821, was so easy to learn that many Cherokee were soon reading and writing in their own language."},"Hammurabi":{"years":"r. c. 1792–1750 BC","from":"Babylon","text":"Hammurabi was a king of Babylon who united much of Mesopotamia under his rule. He is best known for the Code of Hammurabi, one of the oldest surviving written law codes."},"Hector":{"years":"legendary","from":"Troy","text":"In Greek legend, Hector was a prince of Troy and the city's greatest warrior. Homer's Iliad tells how he led the Trojan defense against the Greeks until he fell in battle against Achilles."},"Lysander":{"years":"d. 395 BC","from":"Sparta","text":"Lysander was a Spartan admiral and commander in the Peloponnesian War. He won the decisive naval battle of Aegospotami in 405 BC, which led to the surrender of Athens the following year."},"Ptolemy I Soter":{"years":"c. 367–282 BC","from":"Ptolemaic Egypt","text":"Ptolemy I was a Macedonian general of Alexander the Great who took control of Egypt after Alexander's death. He founded a dynasty that ruled Egypt for nearly 300 years and helped make Alexandria a center of learning."},"Baldwin IV":{"years":"1161–1185","from":"Kingdom of Jerusalem","text":"Baldwin IV was a king of Jerusalem who ruled despite suffering from leprosy from childhood. He is remembered for his courage, leading his army to victory over Saladin at Montgisard in 1177."},"Darius the Great":{"years":"c. 550–486 BC","from":"Achaemenid Persian Empire","text":"Darius I was king of the Persian Empire at its height. He organized it into provinces called satrapies, built roads and a royal capital at Persepolis, and introduced standard coins and taxes."},"Imhotep":{"years":"c. 27th century BC","from":"Old Kingdom Egypt","text":"Imhotep was an official of the pharaoh Djoser and is credited with designing the Step Pyramid at Saqqara, the earliest large stone pyramid. Later Egyptians honored him as a wise man and healer, and eventually as a god."},"Thutmose III":{"years":"r. c. 1479–1425 BC","from":"New Kingdom Egypt","text":"Thutmose III was a pharaoh of Egypt who shared power with Queen Hatshepsut before ruling alone. He led many military campaigns into the Levant, including a victory at Megiddo, greatly expanding Egypt's empire."},"Ashurbanipal":{"years":"r. 669–c. 631 BC","from":"Assyrian Empire","text":"Ashurbanipal was one of the last great kings of the Assyrian Empire. He gathered a vast library of clay tablets at Nineveh, which preserved many Mesopotamian texts, including the Epic of Gilgamesh."},"Ur-Nammu":{"years":"r. c. 2112–2095 BC","from":"Sumer (Third Dynasty of Ur)","text":"Ur-Nammu was a Sumerian king who founded the Third Dynasty of Ur. He is credited with the oldest known surviving law code and with building the great ziggurat of Ur."},"Seleucus I Nicator":{"years":"c. 358–281 BC","from":"Seleucid Empire","text":"Seleucus I was a Macedonian officer of Alexander the Great who won a vast kingdom stretching from Asia Minor to Central Asia. Nicknamed Nicator, meaning Victor, he founded many cities, including Antioch."},"Nur ad-Din":{"years":"1118–1174","from":"Zengid Syria","text":"Nur ad-Din was a Turkish ruler of Syria who made Damascus his capital. He united Muslim Syria against the crusader states and led many campaigns against them, paving the way for Saladin, who served under him."},"Archimedes":{"years":"c. 287–212 BC","from":"Syracuse","text":"Archimedes was a Greek mathematician, inventor and engineer from Syracuse in Sicily. He made major discoveries in geometry and physics and designed machines to defend his city during the Roman siege in which he died."},"Periander":{"years":"r. c. 627–587 BC","from":"Corinth","text":"Periander was a ruler, or tyrant, of Corinth. Ancient writers credited him with making the city rich through trade and public works and sometimes counted him among the Seven Sages of Greece, though they also remembered him as harsh."},"Croesus":{"years":"r. c. 560–546 BC","from":"Kingdom of Lydia","text":"Croesus was a king of Lydia in Asia Minor, famous for his great wealth. Herodotus tells that he gave rich gifts to the oracle at Delphi and consulted it before going to war with Persia, where Cyrus the Great defeated him."},"Minos":{"years":"legendary","from":"Crete","text":"In Greek myth, Minos was a king of Crete and son of Zeus. Legends describe him as a powerful ruler and lawgiver whose palace at Knossos held the Labyrinth, and the Minoan civilization is named after him."},"Aretas IV":{"years":"r. 9 BC–AD 40","from":"Nabataean Kingdom","text":"Aretas IV was king of the Nabataeans, ruling from Petra. During his long reign the kingdom grew rich from the incense trade, and many of Petra's great monuments were built."},"Odaenathus":{"years":"d. 267","from":"Palmyra","text":"Odaenathus was a ruler of Palmyra in Syria and the husband of Zenobia. As an ally of Rome, he won major victories over the Persians and became the leading power in the Roman East before his assassination."},"Hiram I":{"years":"r. c. 980–947 BC","from":"Tyre (Phoenicia)","text":"Hiram I was a Phoenician king of Tyre who expanded the city and its trade by sea. The Bible describes him sending cedar wood and skilled workers to help King Solomon build the Temple in Jerusalem."},"Abdalonymus":{"years":"4th century BC","from":"Sidon (Phoenicia)","text":"Abdalonymus was made king of Sidon after Alexander the Great took the city in 332 BC. Ancient writers say he was a humble gardener of royal descent, chosen for his honesty."},"Shutruk-Nakhunte":{"years":"r. c. 1185–1155 BC","from":"Elam","text":"Shutruk-Nakhunte was a king of Elam who ruled from Susa. He raided Mesopotamia and carried famous monuments back to Susa as trophies, including the stone stele bearing the Code of Hammurabi."},"Shapur I":{"years":"r. c. 240–270","from":"Sasanian Persian Empire","text":"Shapur I was a king of the Sasanian Persian Empire. He won major wars against Rome and captured the Roman emperor Valerian in 260, a triumph he celebrated in great rock carvings."},"Pliny the Elder":{"years":"AD 23–79","from":"Roman Empire","text":"Pliny the Elder was a Roman writer, naturalist and naval commander. He wrote the Natural History, a huge encyclopedia of ancient knowledge, and died near Pompeii during the eruption of Mount Vesuvius in AD 79."},"Stilicho":{"years":"c. 359–408","from":"Western Roman Empire","text":"Stilicho was a general of part-Vandal descent who effectively ran the Western Roman Empire for the young emperor Honorius. He defended Italy against invaders, including Alaric's Visigoths, before falling from favor and being executed in 408."},"Francesco Sforza":{"years":"1401–1466","from":"Duchy of Milan","text":"Francesco Sforza was an Italian mercenary commander, or condottiero, who became Duke of Milan in 1450. A skilled soldier, he founded a ruling family and helped bring a lasting balance of power among the Italian states."},"Roland":{"years":"d. 778","from":"Frankish Kingdom","text":"Roland was a Frankish commander under Charlemagne who died at Roncevaux Pass in 778. Little is known of the real man, but the medieval Song of Roland made him a legendary model of the loyal, brave knight."},"Alfonso X the Wise":{"years":"1221–1284","from":"Kingdom of Castile","text":"Alfonso X was king of Castile and León. Called the Wise, he gathered scholars at Toledo to write and translate works on law, history and astronomy, and sponsored the great law code known as the Siete Partidas."},"Abd al-Rahman III":{"years":"891–961","from":"Caliphate of Córdoba","text":"Abd al-Rahman III was the Umayyad ruler of al-Andalus in Spain who declared himself caliph in 929. He united the region and made Córdoba one of the richest and most cultured cities in Europe."},"Olga of Kyiv":{"years":"d. 969","from":"Kievan Rus'","text":"Olga ruled Kievan Rus' as regent for her young son after her husband, Prince Igor, was killed. Remembered as shrewd and determined, she reorganized how tribute was collected and later became a Christian and a saint."},"Alexander Nevsky":{"years":"1221–1263","from":"Novgorod and Vladimir","text":"Alexander Nevsky was a prince of Novgorod and later Grand Prince of Vladimir. He defeated the Swedes at the Neva River in 1240 and the Teutonic Knights on frozen Lake Peipus in 1242."},"Timur":{"years":"1336–1405","from":"Timurid Empire","text":"Timur, also called Tamerlane, was a Turco-Mongol conqueror who built a vast empire across Central and Western Asia. A formidable military leader, he made Samarkand his capital and filled it with grand buildings."},"Agamemnon":{"years":"legendary","from":"Mycenae","text":"In Greek legend, Agamemnon was king of Mycenae and commander of the Greek army in the Trojan War. Homer's Iliad portrays him as a proud and powerful war leader."},"Romulus":{"years":"legendary","from":"Rome","text":"In Roman legend, Romulus and his twin Remus were raised by a she-wolf, and Romulus founded Rome, traditionally in 753 BC. He was said to be the city's first king and the creator of its army and institutions."},"Theseus":{"years":"legendary","from":"Athens","text":"In Greek myth, Theseus was a hero and king of Athens. He is best known for defeating the Minotaur in the Labyrinth of Crete, and the Athenians credited him with uniting the towns of Attica."},"Dido":{"years":"legendary","from":"Carthage (Phoenician Tyre)","text":"In legend, Dido was a princess of Tyre who fled her homeland and founded Carthage in North Africa. Ancient stories praise her cleverness in gaining land for the city, and the Roman poet Virgil made her a tragic queen in the Aeneid."},"Theodora":{"years":"c. 497–548","from":"Byzantine Empire","text":"Theodora was a Byzantine empress and wife of Justinian I who rose from humble beginnings as an actress. A shrewd adviser, she is credited with persuading Justinian to stand firm during the Nika riots of 532."},"Atlas":{"years":"mythic","from":"Atlantis (legend)","text":"In Greek myth, Atlas was a Titan made to hold up the sky. Plato's legendary tale of Atlantis names its first king Atlas, a son of Poseidon, after whom the island was named."}},"cities":{"Babylon":{"era":"c. 2300 BC – AD 100s","where":"central Iraq","text":"Babylon, on the Euphrates, was the capital of Hammurabi's empire in the 1700s BC and later of Nebuchadnezzar II, who rebuilt it with the Ishtar Gate. It was one of the largest and most famous cities of the ancient world."},"Troy":{"era":"c. 3000 BC – AD 500s","where":"northwestern Turkey","text":"Troy was a real city at Hisarlik, near the Dardanelles, rebuilt many times over three thousand years. Homer's Trojan War is legend; archaeologists have found signs of destruction but cannot prove the war happened as told."},"Sparta":{"era":"height c. 650–371 BC","where":"southern Greece","text":"Sparta was a Greek city-state famous for its strict military training and powerful army. It led the Greeks at Thermopylae in 480 BC and, with the admiral Lysander, defeated Athens in the Peloponnesian War in 404 BC."},"Alexandria":{"era":"founded 331 BC","where":"northern Egypt","text":"Founded by Alexander the Great in 331 BC, Alexandria became the capital of Ptolemaic Egypt. Its Great Library and its lighthouse, one of the Seven Wonders, made it the leading center of learning and trade in the Mediterranean."},"Jerusalem":{"era":"inhabited since c. 3000 BC","where":"Judean Hills, Levant","text":"Jerusalem is one of the world's oldest cities and is sacred to Judaism, Christianity and Islam. Over three thousand years it was ruled by many powers, including the kingdom of Judah, Rome, Islamic caliphates and the Crusaders."},"Persepolis":{"era":"c. 518–330 BC","where":"southern Iran","text":"Darius the Great began Persepolis c. 518 BC as the ceremonial capital of the Persian Empire, the largest empire of its time. Its carved stairways show subject peoples bringing gifts; Alexander the Great burned it in 330 BC."},"Memphis":{"era":"c. 3100 BC – AD 600s","where":"near Cairo, Egypt","text":"Memphis was the capital of Egypt after its unification c. 3100 BC and remained one of its great cities for thousands of years. Nearby stand the pyramids of Giza and Saqqara, including the Step Pyramid designed by Imhotep."},"Thebes":{"era":"height c. 1550–1070 BC","where":"Luxor, Egypt","text":"Thebes was the royal and religious capital of Egypt for much of the New Kingdom, when Egypt was at the height of its power. Its temples at Karnak and Luxor and the royal tombs of the Valley of the Kings lie there."},"Nineveh":{"era":"capital c. 700–612 BC","where":"northern Iraq","text":"Nineveh was the capital of the Assyrian Empire at its height. King Ashurbanipal gathered a great library of clay tablets there, which preserved the Epic of Gilgamesh, before the city fell to the Babylonians and Medes in 612 BC."},"Ur":{"era":"c. 3800–500 BC","where":"southern Iraq","text":"Ur was a great Sumerian city near the Euphrates, grown rich on farming and trade by river and sea. Under Ur-Nammu c. 2100 BC it led an empire, built its great ziggurat and issued one of the oldest known law codes."},"Antioch":{"era":"founded 300 BC","where":"southern Turkey","text":"Founded by Seleucus I in 300 BC, Antioch became the capital of the Seleucid Empire and later one of the Roman Empire's greatest cities. It was an early center of Christianity, where Jesus's followers were first called Christians."},"Damascus":{"era":"ancient – present","where":"Syria","text":"Damascus is among the oldest continuously inhabited cities in the world, built around an oasis at the edge of the desert. It was the capital of the Umayyad Caliphate from AD 661 and later Nur ad-Din's base against the Crusaders."},"Syracuse":{"era":"founded c. 734 BC","where":"Sicily, Italy","text":"Founded by settlers from Corinth c. 734 BC, Syracuse became the most powerful Greek city in Sicily and crushed an Athenian invasion in 413 BC. Archimedes designed machines to defend it before Rome took it in 212 BC."},"Corinth":{"era":"c. 900 BC – AD 500s","where":"southern Greece","text":"Corinth controlled the narrow isthmus joining the Peloponnese to mainland Greece, growing rich from trade between two seas. It flourished under the tyrant Periander c. 600 BC; Rome destroyed it in 146 BC and later rebuilt it."},"Delphi":{"era":"c. 800 BC – AD 390s","where":"central Greece","text":"Delphi was home to the oracle of Apollo, the most respected oracle in the Greek world, where rulers and cities sought advice. Kings such as Croesus of Lydia sent it rich gifts, and it hosted the Pythian Games."},"Knossos":{"era":"palace c. 1900–1350 BC","where":"Crete, Greece","text":"Knossos was the largest Bronze Age site on Crete and the center of the Minoan civilization, with a vast palace. King Minos and the Minotaur's labyrinth are Greek legend, though the palace's maze of rooms may have inspired it."},"Petra":{"era":"c. 300s BC – AD 600s","where":"southern Jordan","text":"Petra was the capital of the Nabataeans, who grew rich controlling caravan routes for incense and spices. Its tombs and temples, carved into rose-red cliffs, peaked under King Aretas IV before Rome annexed the kingdom in AD 106."},"Palmyra":{"era":"height c. AD 1–273","where":"central Syria","text":"Palmyra was a wealthy oasis city on the caravan routes between Rome and Persia. Under Odaenathus and then Queen Zenobia it briefly ruled much of the Roman East, until the emperor Aurelian retook it in AD 272."},"Tyre":{"era":"c. 2700 BC – present","where":"southern Lebanon","text":"Tyre was the leading Phoenician port, famous for its purple dye and for sailors who founded colonies such as Carthage. King Hiram I supplied cedar for Solomon's temple, and the island city held out against Alexander for seven months."},"Sidon":{"era":"c. 3000 BC – present","where":"Lebanon","text":"Sidon was one of the oldest and richest Phoenician cities, known for glassmaking, purple dye and seafaring. Ancient writers say that after it yielded to Alexander the Great in 333 BC, he made Abdalonymus, a humble gardener of royal blood, its king."},"Susa":{"era":"c. 4200 BC – AD 1200s","where":"southwestern Iran","text":"Susa was the capital of Elam for many centuries and later a royal capital of the Persian Empire. The Elamite king Shutruk-Nakhunte carried Hammurabi's law code stele there as war booty, where archaeologists found it in 1901."},"Ctesiphon":{"era":"c. 100s BC – AD 600s","where":"near Baghdad, Iraq","text":"Ctesiphon, on the Tigris, was the capital of the Parthian and then the Sasanian Persian empires for centuries. Shapur I ruled from there after his victories over Rome, and part of its great brick arch, the Taq Kasra, survives."},"Pompeii":{"era":"c. 600s BC – AD 79","where":"near Naples, Italy","text":"Pompeii was a prosperous Roman town buried under volcanic ash when Mount Vesuvius erupted in AD 79. Its preserved streets, homes and paintings show daily Roman life; Pliny the Elder died while leading a rescue by sea."},"Ravenna":{"era":"capital AD 402–751","where":"northeastern Italy","text":"Protected by marshes, Ravenna became the capital of the Western Roman Empire in AD 402 and later of the Ostrogothic kingdom and Byzantine Italy. Its churches hold some of the finest mosaics of the late ancient world."},"Milan":{"era":"c. 600 BC – present","where":"northern Italy","text":"Milan was an imperial capital of the late Roman Empire, where the Edict of Milan granted tolerance to Christians in AD 313. In the Middle Ages it grew rich from trade and arms-making under the Visconti and Sforza dukes."},"Aachen":{"era":"Charlemagne's seat c. AD 794–814","where":"western Germany","text":"Aachen, known for its warm springs, was Charlemagne's favorite residence and the heart of his empire c. AD 800. His Palatine Chapel, now its cathedral, saw the coronations of German kings for some six hundred years."},"Toledo":{"era":"c. 190s BC – present","where":"central Spain","text":"Toledo, on a hill above the Tagus River, was the capital of Visigothic Spain and a major city of Muslim al-Andalus. After Castile took it in 1085, its scholars, backed by Alfonso X, translated Arabic science and philosophy."},"Cordoba":{"era":"height AD 756–1031","where":"southern Spain","text":"Cordoba was the capital of Muslim al-Andalus, and under Abd al-Rahman III, who declared himself caliph in 929, it became one of Europe's largest and most learned cities. Its Great Mosque, with rows of striped arches, still stands."},"Kyiv":{"era":"c. AD 800s – present","where":"northern Ukraine","text":"Kyiv, on the Dnieper River, was the capital of Kievan Rus', a powerful medieval state on the trade route to Constantinople. Princess Olga ruled it as regent and became a Christian; her grandson Vladimir made Christianity the state faith c. 988."},"Novgorod":{"era":"republic c. 1136–1478","where":"northwestern Russia","text":"Novgorod was a wealthy medieval trading republic, linked to the Hanseatic League and governed partly by a town assembly. Its prince Alexander Nevsky defeated the Swedes in 1240 and the Teutonic Knights on frozen Lake Peipus in 1242."},"Samarkand":{"era":"c. 700s BC – present","where":"Uzbekistan","text":"Samarkand was a great oasis city on the Silk Road, known to Alexander the Great as Maracanda. Timur made it his capital in 1370 and filled it with blue-tiled mosques and schools; his grandson Ulugh Beg built a famous observatory there."},"Mycenae":{"era":"height c. 1600–1100 BC","where":"southern Greece","text":"Mycenae was the leading city of Bronze Age Greece, with massive walls, the Lion Gate and royal graves full of gold. Homer made it the home of King Agamemnon, but whether he was a real ruler is unknown."},"Rome":{"era":"founded 753 BC (legend)","where":"central Italy","text":"Legend says Romulus founded Rome in 753 BC. From a small town on the Tiber it grew into the center of an empire stretching from Britain to Egypt, whose roads, laws, Latin language and church shaped Europe."},"Athens":{"era":"golden age 400s BC","where":"Greece","text":"Athens was the leading city of classical Greece, where democracy took shape and the Parthenon was built in the 400s BC. Its thinkers, such as Socrates, Plato and Aristotle, and its playwrights shaped Western philosophy and theater."},"Carthage":{"era":"c. 814–146 BC","where":"near Tunis, Tunisia","text":"Carthage was founded by Phoenicians from Tyre, by tradition under Queen Dido in 814 BC, and became the greatest trading power of the western Mediterranean. It fought three wars with Rome, including Hannibal's, before Rome destroyed it in 146 BC."},"Constantinople":{"era":"AD 330–1453","where":"Istanbul, Turkey","text":"Constantine the Great refounded the Greek town of Byzantium as Constantinople in AD 330. For over a thousand years it was the capital of the Byzantine Empire, guarded by mighty walls and crowned by the Hagia Sophia, until the Ottomans took it in 1453."},"Atlantis":{"era":"legend","where":"beyond the Pillars of Hercules (legend)","text":"Atlantis is an island empire described by the philosopher Plato c. 360 BC as a mighty sea power beyond the Pillars of Hercules. In his story it sank into the sea in a single day; there is no evidence it ever existed."},"Venice":{"era":"lagoon city, 5th c. AD onward","where":"north-east Italy","text":"Built on islands in a lagoon at the head of the Adriatic, Venice grew into a maritime republic led by elected doges. Its merchant fleets dominated trade between Europe and the eastern Mediterranean for centuries."},"Massalia":{"era":"founded c. 600 BC","where":"southern France","text":"Greek settlers from Phocaea founded Massalia around 600 BC, and it became today's Marseille. The colony spread Greek trade, wine and coinage into Gaul for centuries."},"Gades":{"era":"Phoenician foundation","where":"south-west Spain","text":"Gades, today's Cádiz, was founded by Phoenician traders and is often called the oldest city in western Europe. It controlled trade passing beyond the Strait of Gibraltar into the Atlantic."},"London":{"era":"founded c. AD 50","where":"England","text":"The Romans founded Londinium on the Thames soon after their invasion of AD 43. It grew into Britain's largest town and, centuries later, the seat of English kings and a great trading city."},"Trier":{"era":"Roman Augusta Treverorum","where":"western Germany","text":"Founded by the Romans as Augusta Treverorum, Trier became an imperial capital in the late empire. Its great dark gate, the Porta Nigra, still stands."},"Uppsala":{"era":"royal and holy site","where":"Sweden","text":"Old Uppsala was a royal seat of the early Swedes, with great burial mounds and a famous pagan temple. It later became Sweden's archbishopric, and its university was founded in 1477."},"Florence":{"era":"founded 59 BC","where":"central Italy","text":"Founded as the Roman colony of Florentia, Florence became a banking and wool-trading power in the Middle Ages. Under the Medici family it was a leading centre of Renaissance art and learning."},"Bruges":{"era":"trade hub, 13th–15th c.","where":"Belgium","text":"Bruges was one of the richest trading cities of northern Europe in the 13th–15th centuries. Merchants from the Hanseatic towns, Italy and beyond traded cloth and goods along its canals."},"Lugdunum":{"era":"founded 43 BC","where":"eastern France","text":"Founded as a Roman colony in 43 BC, Lugdunum, today's Lyon, became the leading city of Roman Gaul. The emperor Claudius was born there."},"Seville":{"era":"Roman, Moorish and Spanish city","where":"southern Spain","text":"Seville was a major city of Roman and then Moorish Spain, and its Giralda tower began as a minaret. From 1503 it held the royal monopoly on trade with the Americas."},"York":{"era":"Roman Eboracum, Viking Jorvik","where":"northern England","text":"York was the Roman fortress of Eboracum, where Constantine was proclaimed emperor in 306. It later became Jorvik, capital of a Viking kingdom, and then a great medieval city with its Minster."},"Cologne":{"era":"Roman colony AD 50","where":"western Germany","text":"Cologne was founded as the Roman colony of Colonia Agrippina in AD 50. In the Middle Ages it was one of Germany's largest cities, and work on its huge Gothic cathedral began in 1248."},"Genoa":{"era":"maritime republic, c. 1000–1797","where":"north-west Italy","text":"Genoa grew from an ancient Ligurian port into a powerful maritime republic. Its merchants built trading colonies around the Mediterranean and Black Sea, and its bankers later lent heavily to the Spanish crown."},"Birka":{"era":"Viking town, c. 750–975","where":"Sweden","text":"Birka was a Viking Age trading town on an island in Lake Mälaren. The missionary Ansgar visited it around 830, and its traders dealt in goods from as far away as the Arab world."},"Pisa":{"era":"maritime republic, 11th–13th c.","where":"central Italy","text":"Pisa was one of the great maritime republics of medieval Italy, trading across the Mediterranean. Its cathedral square, with the famous leaning bell tower begun in 1173, reflects the wealth of that age."},"Paris":{"era":"Roman Lutetia onward","where":"northern France","text":"Paris began as Lutetia, a Roman town on an island in the Seine. It became the seat of the French kings, and its medieval university and Notre-Dame cathedral made it a centre of learning and faith."},"Granada":{"era":"Nasrid capital 1238–1492","where":"southern Spain","text":"Granada was the capital of the Nasrid emirate, the last Muslim state in Iberia. Its rulers built the Alhambra palace, and the city passed to Ferdinand and Isabella in 1492."},"Winchester":{"era":"capital of Wessex","where":"southern England","text":"Winchester was the chief city of the Anglo-Saxon kingdom of Wessex. Alfred the Great ruled from there, and it remained a royal centre in the years after the Norman Conquest."},"Mainz":{"era":"Roman Mogontiacum","where":"western Germany","text":"Mainz began as the Roman legionary base of Mogontiacum on the Rhine. Around 1450 Johannes Gutenberg developed printing with movable metal type there."},"Hedeby":{"era":"Viking town, 8th–11th c.","where":"northern Germany","text":"Hedeby was a major Viking Age trading town near the base of the Jutland peninsula. It linked trade between the Baltic and North Sea and was protected by the Danevirke earthworks."},"Naples":{"era":"ancient Greek colony","where":"southern Italy","text":"Founded by Greek settlers as Neapolis, meaning “new city”, Naples kept its Greek culture well into Roman times. It later became the capital of a kingdom covering much of southern Italy."},"Avignon":{"era":"papal seat 1309–1377","where":"southern France","text":"From 1309 to 1377 the popes lived in Avignon rather than Rome. Their great Palace of the Popes still dominates the old walled city above the Rhône."},"Lisbon":{"era":"capital from 13th c.","where":"Portugal","text":"Lisbon, an ancient port at the mouth of the Tagus, became Portugal's capital in the 13th century. From here Vasco da Gama and other navigators sailed during the age of exploration."},"Ghent":{"era":"medieval cloth city","where":"Belgium","text":"Ghent was one of the largest cities north of the Alps in the Middle Ages, rich from weaving wool cloth. Its cathedral holds the Ghent Altarpiece, painted by the van Eyck brothers."},"Canterbury":{"era":"mission of 597","where":"south-east England","text":"Augustine's mission from Rome arrived in 597 and made Canterbury the seat of England's leading archbishop. After the death of Thomas Becket in 1170 it became a major pilgrimage site."},"Lübeck":{"era":"refounded 1159","where":"northern Germany","text":"Lübeck, refounded in 1159, became the leading city of the Hanseatic League. Its merchants traded across the Baltic and North Sea, and Hanseatic assemblies usually met there."},"Palermo":{"era":"founded by Phoenicians, 8th c. BC","where":"Sicily, Italy","text":"Founded by Phoenician traders, Palermo later passed to Romans, Byzantines, Arabs and Normans. Under the Norman kings of Sicily in the 12th century it was a rich capital blending Latin, Greek and Arabic culture."},"Roskilde":{"era":"royal seat, 10th c. onward","where":"Denmark","text":"Roskilde was an early royal and church centre of Denmark, and its cathedral holds the tombs of many Danish monarchs. Five Viking ships found sunk in its fjord are displayed there."},"Reims":{"era":"coronation city","where":"north-east France","text":"Reims was where Clovis, king of the Franks, was baptised, traditionally around 496. For centuries afterwards most French kings were crowned in its cathedral."},"Tarraco":{"era":"Roman provincial capital","where":"north-east Spain","text":"Tarraco, today's Tarragona, was one of the oldest Roman bases in Spain. It became capital of the vast province of Hispania Tarraconensis, and its amphitheatre and walls survive."},"Verona":{"era":"Roman city, 1st c. BC","where":"northern Italy","text":"Verona stood where Roman roads crossed the Adige river, and its Roman amphitheatre still hosts performances today. In the Middle Ages the city was ruled by the della Scala family."},"Bath":{"era":"Roman Aquae Sulis","where":"south-west England","text":"The Romans built baths and a temple at the hot springs they called Aquae Sulis. The site later grew into the town of Bath, with a great medieval abbey."},"Nuremberg":{"era":"free imperial city","where":"southern Germany","text":"Nuremberg was a free imperial city of the Holy Roman Empire and a centre of crafts and trade. The artist Albrecht Dürer lived and worked there around 1500."},"Padua":{"era":"university founded 1222","where":"north-east Italy","text":"Padua was a prosperous Roman town and later a free medieval city. Its university, founded in 1222, became famous for medicine and science, and Galileo taught there."},"Arles":{"era":"Roman city, 1st c. BC","where":"southern France","text":"Arles was an important Roman city on the Rhône, made a colony under Julius Caesar. Its amphitheatre and theatre still stand, and it remained a leading city of late Roman Gaul."},"Mérida":{"era":"founded 25 BC","where":"western Spain","text":"Founded in 25 BC as Augusta Emerita for retired Roman soldiers, Mérida became capital of the province of Lusitania. Its Roman theatre, bridge and aqueducts are among the best preserved in Spain."},"Nidaros":{"era":"founded c. 997","where":"Norway","text":"Nidaros, today's Trondheim, was founded by King Olaf Tryggvason around 997. Its cathedral, built over the grave of St Olaf, was the main pilgrimage site of medieval Norway."},"Bologna":{"era":"university c. 1088","where":"northern Italy","text":"Bologna's university, traditionally dated to 1088, is often called the oldest in continuous operation in the Western world. Students came from across Europe to study law there."},"Colchester":{"era":"Roman Camulodunum","where":"eastern England","text":"As Camulodunum, Colchester was a tribal capital and then the first Roman colony in Britain. It was burned during Boudica's revolt around AD 60 and afterwards rebuilt."},"Worms":{"era":"Diet of 1521","where":"western Germany","text":"Worms is one of Germany's oldest cities and appears in the medieval Song of the Nibelungs. In 1521 Martin Luther defended his writings before the emperor at the Diet of Worms."},"Antwerp":{"era":"great port, 16th c.","where":"Belgium","text":"In the 16th century Antwerp became the busiest port and money market in Europe. Spices, silver and cloth from across the world passed through its exchange."},"Alesia":{"era":"siege of 52 BC","where":"eastern France","text":"Alesia was a hilltop stronghold of the Gaulish Mandubii tribe. In 52 BC Julius Caesar besieged Vercingetorix there, and his surrender ended large-scale Gaulish resistance to Rome."},"Numantia":{"era":"fell 133 BC","where":"northern Spain","text":"Numantia was a Celtiberian hill town that resisted Rome for years. It fell in 133 BC after a long siege by Scipio Aemilianus, and its name became a byword for stubborn defence."},"Siena":{"era":"medieval republic","where":"central Italy","text":"Siena was an independent republic and banking centre that rivalled Florence in the 13th and 14th centuries. Its Palio horse race, run around the main square, has medieval roots."},"Bergen":{"era":"founded c. 1070","where":"Norway","text":"Bergen was founded around 1070 and served for a time as Norway's capital. The Hanseatic League kept a major trading office there, exchanging grain for dried cod."},"Edinburgh":{"era":"royal capital, medieval","where":"Scotland","text":"Edinburgh grew around a fortress on an ancient volcanic rock. By the late Middle Ages it was the capital of the Kingdom of Scotland, with the Royal Mile linking castle and palace."},"Augsburg":{"era":"founded c. 15 BC","where":"southern Germany","text":"Augsburg was founded as a Roman camp around 15 BC and named after Augustus. In the 16th century it was home to the Fugger banking family, the richest in Europe."},"Ostia":{"era":"port of Rome, from 4th c. BC","where":"central Italy","text":"Ostia stood at the mouth of the Tiber and served as the main harbour of ancient Rome. Grain ships unloaded there, and its well-preserved ruins show apartment blocks, shops and warehouses."},"Orléans":{"era":"siege lifted 1429","where":"central France","text":"Orléans controlled an important crossing of the Loire. In 1429 Joan of Arc helped lift an English siege of the city, a turning point in the Hundred Years' War."},"Saguntum":{"era":"besieged 219 BC","where":"eastern Spain","text":"Saguntum was an Iberian town allied with Rome. Hannibal's capture of it in 219 BC led directly to the Second Punic War."},"Visby":{"era":"Baltic trade town, medieval","where":"Gotland, Sweden","text":"Visby on the island of Gotland was a leading Baltic trading town in the Middle Ages. Its long town wall, with dozens of towers, still survives almost complete."},"Veii":{"era":"Etruscan city, taken 396 BC","where":"central Italy","text":"Veii was one of the richest Etruscan cities, standing a short distance north of Rome. After a long war, Rome captured it in 396 BC, a key step in Roman expansion."},"Oxford":{"era":"teaching from late 11th c.","where":"southern England","text":"Oxford grew at a ford on the Thames and was a town by the 10th century. Teaching there is recorded from the late 11th century, making its university the oldest in the English-speaking world."},"Vienna":{"era":"Roman Vindobona; Habsburg seat","where":"Austria","text":"Vienna grew from the Roman camp of Vindobona on the Danube. It became the seat of the Habsburg rulers and withstood an Ottoman siege in 1529."},"Carcassonne":{"era":"walled city, Roman to medieval","where":"southern France","text":"Carcassonne is a hilltop city ringed by double walls and dozens of towers. Its fortifications grew from Roman and medieval foundations and were heavily restored in the 19th century."},"Salamanca":{"era":"university founded 1218","where":"western Spain","text":"Salamanca's university, founded in 1218, is one of the oldest in Europe. In the 16th century its scholars led debates on law, economics and the rights of peoples in the Americas."},"Utrecht":{"era":"bishopric from 8th c.","where":"Netherlands","text":"Utrecht began as a Roman fort on the Rhine and became the seat of a powerful bishop from the 8th century. The Union of Utrecht in 1579 joined the northern Dutch provinces together."},"Aquileia":{"era":"founded 181 BC","where":"north-east Italy","text":"Aquileia was founded as a Roman colony in 181 BC near the head of the Adriatic. It grew into one of the largest cities of the empire and later an important early Christian centre."},"Reykjavík":{"era":"settled c. 874","where":"Iceland","text":"According to Icelandic tradition, Ingólfr Arnarson made the first permanent settlement in Iceland at Reykjavík around 874. For centuries it remained a farm before growing into a town."},"Dublin":{"era":"Norse foundation c. 841","where":"Ireland","text":"Norse settlers founded a fortified base at Dublin around 841 and built it into a busy trading town. After 1170 it became the centre of English rule in Ireland."},"Prague":{"era":"Bohemian capital","where":"Czech Republic","text":"Prague grew beneath its castle as the capital of Bohemia. Under Charles IV in the 14th century it was the seat of the Holy Roman Emperor, who founded its university in 1348."},"Tours":{"era":"shrine of St Martin","where":"western France","text":"Tours grew around the shrine of St Martin, a 4th-century bishop, and became a major pilgrimage site. The scholar Alcuin later ran a famous school and scriptorium at its abbey."},"Barcelona":{"era":"Roman Barcino onward","where":"north-east Spain","text":"Barcelona grew from the Roman colony of Barcino. As the chief city of the Crown of Aragon it ran a Mediterranean trading network, and its Book of the Consulate of the Sea set out maritime law."},"Amalfi":{"era":"maritime republic, 9th–12th c.","where":"southern Italy","text":"Amalfi was a small but wealthy maritime republic on the cliffs south of Naples. Its sailors traded across the Mediterranean, and its sea laws, the Tabula Amalfitana, were widely used."},"Ragusa":{"era":"merchant republic to 1808","where":"Croatia","text":"Ragusa, today's Dubrovnik, was a self-governing merchant republic on the Adriatic coast. Skilled diplomacy and trade kept it independent for centuries, and its stone walls still encircle the old town."},"Argos":{"era":"Bronze Age onward","where":"southern Greece","text":"Argos is one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in Greece. In myth it was the home of heroes such as Diomedes, and in history it was a long rival of Sparta in the Peloponnese."},"Trebizond":{"era":"empire 1204–1461","where":"north-east Turkey","text":"Trebizond began as a Greek colony on the Black Sea coast. From 1204 to 1461 it was the capital of the Empire of Trebizond, a Byzantine successor state rich from trade with Persia."},"Kraków":{"era":"royal capital, medieval","where":"Poland","text":"Kraków was the royal capital of Poland, where kings were crowned in Wawel Cathedral. Its university, founded in 1364, later taught the young Nicolaus Copernicus."},"Chersonesus":{"era":"founded 5th c. BC","where":"Crimea","text":"Chersonesus was founded by Greek colonists in south-western Crimea in the 5th century BC. It remained a Byzantine outpost for centuries, and its ruins preserve a grid of streets and houses."},"Mtskheta":{"era":"ancient royal capital","where":"Georgia","text":"Mtskheta was the capital of the ancient Georgian kingdom of Iberia. Christianity became the state religion there in the 4th century, and its Svetitskhoveli Cathedral remains a major holy site."},"Salona":{"era":"Roman provincial capital","where":"Croatia","text":"Salona was the capital of the Roman province of Dalmatia and one of its largest cities. The emperor Diocletian built his retirement palace nearby, around which the city of Split later grew."},"Pella":{"era":"capital from c. 400 BC","where":"northern Greece","text":"Pella became the capital of the kingdom of Macedon around 400 BC. Alexander the Great was born there, and its houses have some of the finest pebble mosaics of the ancient world."},"Sinope":{"era":"Greek colony","where":"northern Turkey","text":"Sinope was a Greek colony on the north coast of Anatolia and the chief harbour of the southern Black Sea. The philosopher Diogenes the Cynic was born there."},"Gniezno":{"era":"early Polish capital","where":"Poland","text":"Gniezno was an early capital of the Polish state. An archbishopric was founded there in the year 1000, and it saw the first Polish royal coronation in 1025."},"Sirmium":{"era":"Roman imperial city","where":"Serbia","text":"Sirmium on the Sava river was one of the capitals of the Roman Empire in the late 3rd and 4th centuries. Several emperors were born in or near the city."},"Thessalonica":{"era":"founded 315 BC","where":"northern Greece","text":"Thessalonica was founded in 315 BC by King Cassander of Macedon. A key port on the Roman Via Egnatia, it later became the second city of the Byzantine Empire."},"Ephesus":{"era":"Ionian and Roman city","where":"western Turkey","text":"Ephesus was a great port city of Ionia and home to the Temple of Artemis, one of the Seven Wonders. Under Rome it was among the largest cities of Asia Minor."},"Vilnius":{"era":"first recorded 1323","where":"Lithuania","text":"Vilnius was the capital of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania, first mentioned in writing in 1323. Catholic, Orthodox and Jewish communities lived there, and its university was founded in 1579."},"Singidunum":{"era":"Roman fortress, became Belgrade","where":"Serbia","text":"Singidunum was a Celtic settlement and then a Roman fortress where the Sava meets the Danube. It later became Belgrade, a fortress city contested for centuries by Byzantines, Hungarians and Ottomans."},"Panticapaeum":{"era":"Bosporan capital","where":"Crimea","text":"Panticapaeum, today's Kerch, was founded by Greeks from Miletus. It became the capital of the Bosporan Kingdom, which shipped grain from the Black Sea to Athens."},"Tbilisi":{"era":"founded 5th c. AD","where":"Georgia","text":"Tbilisi was founded in the 5th century by King Vakhtang Gorgasali, and its name comes from its warm springs. It became the Georgian capital and flourished under Queen Tamar around 1200."},"Olympia":{"era":"games from 776 BC","where":"southern Greece","text":"Olympia was a sanctuary of Zeus in the western Peloponnese. The Olympic Games were held there every four years, traditionally from 776 BC, and its temple held a famous gold and ivory statue of Zeus."},"Pergamon":{"era":"Attalid capital, 3rd–2nd c. BC","where":"western Turkey","text":"Pergamon was the capital of the Attalid kings in the 3rd and 2nd centuries BC. Its library rivalled that of Alexandria, and its Great Altar is now in a Berlin museum."},"Riga":{"era":"founded 1201","where":"Latvia","text":"Riga was founded in 1201 by Bishop Albert near the mouth of the Daugava river. It joined the Hanseatic League and became a leading port of the eastern Baltic."},"Serdica":{"era":"Roman city, became Sofia","where":"Bulgaria","text":"Serdica was a Thracian and then Roman city, much favoured by Constantine the Great. It hosted a church council in 343 and later became Sofia, the Bulgarian capital."},"Rhodes":{"era":"founded 408 BC","where":"Aegean islands, Greece","text":"The city of Rhodes was founded in 408 BC and grew rich as a trading port. Its huge bronze Colossus was one of the Seven Wonders, and the Knights Hospitaller later ruled the island."},"Miletus":{"era":"Ionian city","where":"western Turkey","text":"Miletus was a leading Ionian city that founded many colonies around the Black Sea. Thales and other early Greek philosophers lived there, beginning a new tradition of explaining nature."},"Pskov":{"era":"medieval trading city","where":"north-west Russia","text":"Pskov was a fortified trading city in the far north-west of the Rus lands. For much of the 14th and 15th centuries it was a self-governing republic with its own assembly."},"Philippopolis":{"era":"Thracian and Roman city","where":"Bulgaria","text":"Philippopolis, today's Plovdiv, is among the oldest continuously inhabited cities in Europe. It took its Greek name from Philip II of Macedon and has a well-preserved Roman theatre."},"Olbia":{"era":"Greek colony, 6th c. BC","where":"southern Ukraine","text":"Olbia was founded by Greeks from Miletus near the mouth of the Southern Bug river. It traded with the Scythians, and Herodotus described the region and its peoples."},"Kutaisi":{"era":"ancient and medieval capital","where":"Georgia","text":"Kutaisi is one of the oldest cities in Georgia and was a capital of the united Georgian kingdom. King David the Builder founded the nearby Gelati Monastery and academy in 1106."},"Mystras":{"era":"castle built 1249","where":"southern Greece","text":"Mystras grew below a castle built in 1249 near ancient Sparta. In the 14th and 15th centuries it was capital of the Byzantine Despotate of the Morea and a centre of late Byzantine learning."},"Halicarnassus":{"era":"Carian city","where":"south-west Turkey","text":"Halicarnassus, today's Bodrum, was the birthplace of the historian Herodotus. The tomb of the Carian ruler Mausolus, one of the Seven Wonders, gave us the word mausoleum."},"Suzdal":{"era":"Rus town, 11th c. onward","where":"central Russia","text":"Suzdal was one of the oldest towns of north-eastern Rus and a seat of its princes. Its many monasteries and white stone churches made it an important religious centre."},"Tarnovo":{"era":"capital 1185–1393","where":"Bulgaria","text":"Tarnovo was the capital of the Second Bulgarian Empire from 1185 to 1393. Its royal palace and patriarchate stood on the fortified Tsarevets hill above the Yantra river."},"Pylos":{"era":"Mycenaean palace","where":"southern Greece","text":"Pylos was the seat of a Mycenaean palace linked to King Nestor of Homer's epics. Clay tablets found there preserve early Greek written in the Linear B script."},"Sardis":{"era":"Lydian capital","where":"western Turkey","text":"Sardis was the capital of the kingdom of Lydia, ruled by the wealthy King Croesus. The Lydians are credited with some of the earliest coins, made of electrum and gold."},"Preslav":{"era":"capital from 893","where":"Bulgaria","text":"Preslav became the capital of the First Bulgarian Empire in 893. It was a centre of Old Bulgarian literature and art, known for its painted ceramic icons."},"Moscow":{"era":"first recorded 1147","where":"Russia","text":"Moscow is first mentioned in a chronicle in 1147. Its princes rose to lead the Rus lands, and the Kremlin at its heart became the seat of the grand princes and tsars."},"Dodona":{"era":"oracle of Zeus","where":"north-west Greece","text":"Dodona in Epirus held the oldest oracle in Greece, according to Herodotus. Priests interpreted the will of Zeus from the rustling of a sacred oak tree."},"Gordion":{"era":"Phrygian capital","where":"central Turkey","text":"Gordion was the capital of Phrygia, linked in legend with King Midas. Alexander the Great is said to have cut the Gordian Knot there in 333 BC."},"Kaffa":{"era":"Genoese colony, late 13th c.","where":"Crimea","text":"Kaffa, on the site of ancient Theodosia, was the main Genoese trading colony on the Black Sea from the late 13th century. Its port flourished until it passed to the Ottomans in 1475."},"Ani":{"era":"Armenian capital, 10th–11th c.","where":"eastern Turkey","text":"Ani was the capital of the Bagratid kingdom of Armenia and was called the city of a thousand and one churches. Its ruined walls and cathedral stand on a plateau above a river gorge."},"Ohrid":{"era":"centre of learning, 9th c.","where":"North Macedonia","text":"Ohrid, on the lake of the same name, became a centre of Slavic literacy through St Clement and St Naum in the late 9th century. It was later the seat of an influential archbishopric."},"Chernihiv":{"era":"Rus city, 10th c.","where":"northern Ukraine","text":"Chernihiv was one of the leading cities of Kyivan Rus and the seat of a powerful principality. Its Cathedral of the Transfiguration, begun in the 11th century, is among the oldest surviving Rus churches."},"Eretria":{"era":"Archaic trading city","where":"Euboea, Greece","text":"Eretria on the island of Euboea was an early Greek trading power that founded colonies in Italy and the north Aegean. It supported the Ionian Revolt and was sacked by the Persians in 490 BC."},"Hattusa":{"era":"Hittite capital, 2nd millennium BC","where":"central Turkey","text":"Hattusa was the capital of the Hittite Empire in the second millennium BC. Thousands of clay tablets found there include treaties, laws and letters to Egypt and Babylon."},"Dyrrachium":{"era":"founded 627 BC","where":"Albania","text":"Dyrrachium, today's Durrës, was founded by Greek colonists in 627 BC as Epidamnos. It was the western start of the Via Egnatia, the Roman road leading east to Byzantium."},"Polotsk":{"era":"first recorded 862","where":"Belarus","text":"Polotsk, first mentioned in the chronicles under 862, was the seat of an early principality on the Western Dvina. Its Cathedral of Holy Wisdom dates from the 11th century."},"Epidaurus":{"era":"sanctuary of Asclepius","where":"southern Greece","text":"Epidaurus was famous for its sanctuary of Asclepius, god of healing, where the sick came seeking cures. Its theatre, built in the 4th century BC, is known for excellent acoustics."},"Nicaea":{"era":"council of 325","where":"north-west Turkey","text":"Nicaea hosted the first ecumenical council of the Christian church in 325, which produced the Nicene Creed. Later it was a Seljuk capital and then seat of the Byzantine Empire of Nicaea."},"Sarmizegetusa":{"era":"Dacian capital, fell AD 106","where":"Romania","text":"Sarmizegetusa Regia was the mountain capital of the Dacian kingdom. It fell to the Roman emperor Trajan in AD 106, and Dacia became a Roman province."},"Tanais":{"era":"Greek trading city","where":"Don delta, Russia","text":"Tanais was a Greek trading city near the mouth of the Don river, at the far north-eastern edge of the Greek world. It linked Bosporan merchants with the nomadic peoples of the steppe."},"Erebuni":{"era":"founded 782 BC","where":"Armenia","text":"Erebuni was founded in 782 BC by King Argishti I of Urartu, as an inscription records. The fortress gave its name to Yerevan, the city that grew up around it."},"Staraya Ladoga":{"era":"founded mid-8th c.","where":"north-west Russia","text":"Staraya Ladoga, founded in the mid-8th century, was one of the earliest towns of the Rus lands. Scandinavian and Slavic traders met there on the route from the Baltic to the rivers of the east."},"Aegina":{"era":"island trading state","where":"Saronic Gulf, Greece","text":"Aegina was an island trading state near Athens and one of the first Greek cities to mint coins. Its silver “turtle” coins circulated widely across the Greek world."},"Konya":{"era":"Seljuk capital","where":"central Turkey","text":"Konya, ancient Iconium, was the capital of the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum. The poet and mystic Rumi lived and taught there in the 13th century."},"Tomis":{"era":"Greek colony","where":"Romania","text":"Tomis was a Greek colony on the Black Sea coast, today's Constanța. The Roman poet Ovid spent his final years there in exile."},"Bolghar":{"era":"Volga Bulgar capital","where":"middle Volga, Russia","text":"Bolghar was the capital of Volga Bulgaria, a trading state on the middle Volga. Its ruler accepted Islam in 922, the year the traveller Ibn Fadlan visited."},"Delos":{"era":"sacred island","where":"Cyclades, Greece","text":"Delos was held sacred as the birthplace of Apollo and Artemis. It later became a busy free port and was for a time the treasury of the Delian League."},"Bursa":{"era":"Ottoman capital from 1326","where":"north-west Turkey","text":"Bursa, taken in 1326, became the first major capital of the Ottoman state. It grew rich from the silk trade, and early sultans were buried in its mosques and tombs."},"Adrianople":{"era":"refounded by Hadrian","where":"European Turkey","text":"Adrianople in Thrace was refounded by the emperor Hadrian and named after him. As Edirne it later served as an Ottoman capital, and its Selimiye Mosque is a masterpiece of the architect Sinan."},"Phasis":{"era":"Greek colony in Colchis","where":"western Georgia","text":"Phasis was a Greek colony at the mouth of the river of the same name in Colchis. In Greek myth, Jason and the Argonauts sailed to Colchis in search of the Golden Fleece."},"Derbent":{"era":"Sasanian walls, 6th c.","where":"Dagestan, Russia","text":"Derbent guards a narrow pass between the Caucasus Mountains and the Caspian Sea. Its great stone walls, built by the Sasanian Persians in the 6th century, controlled the route north to the steppe."},"Smolensk":{"era":"Rus city, 9th c. onward","where":"western Russia","text":"Smolensk on the upper Dnieper was an early Rus city on the trade route from the Baltic to the Black Sea. In 1609–1611 it withstood a long Polish siege."},"Philippi":{"era":"founded 356 BC","where":"northern Greece","text":"Philippi was founded by Philip II of Macedon in 356 BC near gold mines. In 42 BC Mark Antony and Octavian won a decisive battle there, and the apostle Paul later founded a Christian community in the city."},"Kanesh":{"era":"trading city, c. 1900 BC","where":"central Turkey","text":"Kanesh, at the site of Kültepe, was a Bronze Age city with a quarter for Assyrian merchants. Their thousands of clay tablets, from about 1900 BC, record trade in tin and textiles."},"Buda":{"era":"royal seat, 13th c. onward","where":"Hungary","text":"Buda, on the hills above the Danube, became the royal seat of the Kingdom of Hungary. Under King Matthias Corvinus in the 15th century its court was a centre of Renaissance learning."},"Marrakesh":{"era":"Almoravid–Almohad, 11th–13th c.","where":"Morocco","text":"The Almoravids founded Marrakesh c. 1070 as their capital in southern Morocco. Under them and the Almohads it grew into a major center of trade and learning, crowned by the Koutoubia Mosque."},"Tenochtitlan":{"era":"Mexica, c. 1325–1521","where":"Mexico","text":"The Mexica founded Tenochtitlan c. 1325 on an island in Lake Texcoco. Linked to the shore by causeways and fed by chinampa gardens, it grew into an imperial capital and one of the world's largest cities."},"Cairo":{"era":"Fatimid–Mamluk, from 969","where":"Egypt","text":"The Fatimid dynasty founded Cairo in 969 beside the older town of Fustat. With the al-Azhar mosque and university, it became one of the largest and most influential cities of the medieval world."},"Timbuktu":{"era":"Mali–Songhai, 14th–16th c.","where":"Mali","text":"Timbuktu, near the Niger River, grew from a seasonal camp into a famous city of trade and scholarship. Its Djinguereber and Sankore mosques and its many manuscripts drew scholars from across the Islamic world."},"Kilwa":{"era":"Swahili, 12th–15th c.","where":"Tanzania","text":"Kilwa Kisiwani, an island off southern Tanzania, became the richest Swahili city by controlling the gold trade from the south. Its Great Mosque and Husuni Kubwa palace were built of coral stone."},"Aksum":{"era":"Aksumite, c. 1st–7th c.","where":"Ethiopia","text":"Aksum in northern Ethiopia was the capital of a powerful trading kingdom that minted its own coins. Its rulers raised tall carved stone stelae, and King Ezana adopted Christianity in the 4th century."},"Cusco":{"era":"Inca, 15th–16th c.","where":"Peru","text":"Cusco in the Peruvian Andes was the capital of the Inca, who called their realm Tawantinsuyu. Its finely fitted stone walls, the Coricancha temple and Sacsayhuamán reflect Inca mastery of building."},"Kerma":{"era":"Kingdom of Kerma, c. 2500–1500 BC","where":"Sudan","text":"Kerma in northern Sudan was the capital of the first great Kushite kingdom, flourishing c. 2500–1500 BC. Its builders raised the massive mud-brick temple known as the Western Deffufa."},"Teotihuacan":{"era":"c. 100 BC–AD 550","where":"Mexico","text":"Teotihuacan in the Valley of Mexico flourished c. 100 BC–AD 550 and was among the largest cities of its time. Its builders laid out the Avenue of the Dead and the Pyramids of the Sun and Moon."},"Fez":{"era":"Idrisid–Marinid, 9th–15th c.","where":"Morocco","text":"Founded by the Idrisid dynasty around 789–809, Fez became a leading city of Morocco. Its al-Qarawiyyin mosque, founded in 859 by Fatima al-Fihri, grew into one of the world's oldest centers of learning."},"Great Zimbabwe":{"era":"Shona, c. 11th–15th c.","where":"Zimbabwe","text":"Great Zimbabwe was the capital of a Shona state on the Zimbabwe plateau, flourishing from about the 11th to the 15th century. Its people built massive dry-stone walls without mortar and traded gold and ivory with the coast."},"Cahokia":{"era":"Mississippian, c. 1050–1200","where":"Illinois, USA","text":"Cahokia near the Mississippi River was the largest city of the Mississippian culture, peaking c. 1050–1200. Its people built Monks Mound, the largest earthen mound in North America."},"Djenné":{"era":"From c. 250 BC","where":"Mali","text":"Djenné in the inland Niger Delta was a major market town linking desert, savanna and forest trade. It is renowned for mud-brick architecture, and the nearby site of Djenné-Djeno is far older."},"Tikal":{"era":"Classic Maya","where":"Guatemala","text":"Tikal in the rainforest of northern Guatemala was one of the greatest Maya cities of the Classic period. Its tall stepped temples rise above the trees, and its rulers recorded their history in carved inscriptions."},"Machu Picchu":{"era":"Inca, 15th c.","where":"Peru","text":"Machu Picchu was built in the 15th century as a royal estate on a ridge above the Urubamba River. Its terraces, temples and houses show the Inca skill of fitting buildings to steep mountain landscapes."},"Mombasa":{"era":"Swahili city-state","where":"Kenya","text":"Mombasa was a prosperous Swahili city-state trading across the Indian Ocean. Its island harbor made it a prize, and the Portuguese built Fort Jesus there in 1593."},"Kairouan":{"era":"Early Islamic, from c. 670","where":"Tunisia","text":"Arab forces founded Kairouan c. 670 as a military and administrative base. Its Great Mosque made it one of the most important religious and scholarly cities of the Islamic west."},"Amarna":{"era":"New Kingdom, 14th c. BC","where":"Egypt","text":"Pharaoh Akhenaten built Amarna c. 1346 BC as a new capital devoted to the sun disc, the Aten. It was abandoned soon after his death, leaving distinctive art and the diplomatic archive known as the Amarna letters."},"Palenque":{"era":"Classic Maya","where":"Mexico","text":"Palenque in Chiapas was a Classic Maya city known for refined architecture and sculpture. The Temple of the Inscriptions holds the tomb of its ruler K'inich Janaab Pakal."},"Adulis":{"era":"Aksumite port","where":"Eritrea","text":"Adulis on the Red Sea coast was the main port of the Aksumite kingdom. Ivory and other goods passed through it on trade routes linking Africa with Arabia, India and the Mediterranean."},"Gao":{"era":"Songhai, 15th–16th c.","where":"Mali","text":"Gao on the Niger River was the capital of the Songhai Empire, one of the largest states in African history. The Tomb of Askia, a pyramid-shaped mud-brick structure, dates from the late 15th century."},"Napata":{"era":"Kushite, 8th–4th c. BC","where":"Sudan","text":"Napata, near the sacred mountain Jebel Barkal, was a capital of the Kingdom of Kush. Its kings ruled Egypt as the 25th Dynasty in the 8th and 7th centuries BC."},"Copán":{"era":"Classic Maya","where":"Honduras","text":"Copán in western Honduras was a Classic Maya kingdom famed for its sculpture and writing. Its Hieroglyphic Stairway carries the longest known Maya inscription, recording the city's royal history."},"Chaco Canyon":{"era":"Ancestral Puebloan, c. 850–1150","where":"New Mexico, USA","text":"Chaco Canyon was a major center of the Ancestral Puebloan world c. 850–1150. Its people built great houses such as Pueblo Bonito and linked them with long, straight roads."},"Chan Chan":{"era":"Chimú, c. 900–1470","where":"Peru","text":"Chan Chan near Trujillo was the capital of the Chimú kingdom. Built of adobe and decorated with patterned reliefs, it was among the largest cities in the Americas before the Inca absorbed the Chimú."},"Leptis Magna":{"era":"Phoenician–Roman","where":"Libya","text":"Founded by Phoenicians on the Libyan coast, Leptis Magna grew rich under Rome from olive oil and trade. Emperor Septimius Severus, born there, rebuilt it with a grand forum, basilica and harbor."},"Mapungubwe":{"era":"c. 1075–1220","where":"South Africa","text":"Mapungubwe, near where South Africa, Botswana and Zimbabwe meet, was the capital of an early southern African kingdom. Royal graves on its hilltop held gold objects, including a famous small gold rhinoceros."},"Malindi":{"era":"Swahili, 14th–16th c.","where":"Kenya","text":"Malindi was a Swahili city-state with trade links to Arabia, India and China. Its envoys brought a giraffe to the Ming court, and in 1498 it welcomed Vasco da Gama."},"Koumbi Saleh":{"era":"Ghana Empire, c. 9th–12th c.","where":"Mauritania","text":"Koumbi Saleh in southern Mauritania is widely thought to have been the capital of the Ghana Empire. Its stone houses and mosques reflect a city enriched by trans-Saharan trade in gold and salt."},"Calakmul":{"era":"Classic Maya","where":"Mexico","text":"Calakmul in Campeche was a seat of the Kaanul, or Snake, dynasty in the Classic period. Its kings led a wide network of allies and were long the great rivals of Tikal."},"Abydos":{"era":"Early Dynastic–New Kingdom","where":"Egypt","text":"Abydos in Upper Egypt held tombs of Egypt's earliest kings and became the chief cult center of the god Osiris. Pilgrims visited for centuries, and Seti I built a celebrated temple there."},"Harar":{"era":"Adal Sultanate, 16th c.","where":"Ethiopia","text":"Harar in eastern Ethiopia became a leading center of Islamic learning and trade in the Horn of Africa. In the 16th century it was enclosed by the Jugol wall and served as capital of the Adal Sultanate."},"Cyrene":{"era":"Greek–Roman, from c. 631 BC","where":"Libya","text":"Greek settlers from the island of Thera founded Cyrene c. 631 BC in eastern Libya. It prospered on grain, horses and the prized silphium plant, and became a noted center of philosophy and scholarship."},"Tiwanaku":{"era":"c. 500–1000","where":"Bolivia","text":"Tiwanaku near Lake Titicaca was the center of a major Andean state c. 500–1000. Its builders raised precise stone platforms and gateways, including the Gateway of the Sun."},"Chichen Itza":{"era":"Maya, c. 600–1200","where":"Mexico","text":"Chichen Itza in Yucatán was a major Maya city of the Late Classic and Early Postclassic periods. Its stepped pyramid, El Castillo, casts a serpent-like shadow at the equinoxes, and its Sacred Cenote received offerings."},"Kano":{"era":"Hausa, from c. 1000","where":"Nigeria","text":"Kano in northern Nigeria was one of the great Hausa city-states, enclosed by long earthen walls. Its markets, dye pits and leather crafts made it a major center of trans-Saharan trade."},"Mesa Verde":{"era":"Ancestral Puebloan, c. 600–1300","where":"Colorado, USA","text":"Ancestral Puebloan people lived at Mesa Verde in southwestern Colorado for centuries. In the 1200s they built cliff dwellings such as Cliff Palace in sandstone alcoves before moving south."},"Gedi":{"era":"Swahili, c. 12th–17th c.","where":"Kenya","text":"Gedi was a walled Swahili town near the Kenyan coast, flourishing roughly from the 12th to the 17th century. Its ruins include a palace, mosques and stone houses with imported Chinese porcelain."},"Meroë":{"era":"Kushite, to 4th c. AD","where":"Sudan","text":"Meroë was a capital of the Kingdom of Kush for many centuries until the 4th century AD. Its people built hundreds of steep pyramids, worked iron on a large scale and wrote in their own Meroitic script."},"Uxmal":{"era":"Late Classic Maya","where":"Mexico","text":"Uxmal in the Puuc hills of Yucatán was a leading Maya city of the Late Classic period. Its buildings show the Puuc style of fine stone mosaics, seen at the Pyramid of the Magician and the Nunnery Quadrangle."},"Volubilis":{"era":"Mauretanian–Roman","where":"Morocco","text":"Volubilis was an important town of the Mauretanian kingdom and later a Roman provincial city in northern Morocco. Its olive presses and mosaics survive, and Idris I made it his base in the late 8th century."},"Khami":{"era":"Torwa state, 15th–17th c.","where":"Zimbabwe","text":"Khami in western Zimbabwe was the capital of the Torwa state after the decline of Great Zimbabwe. Its builders created terraced stone platforms faced with finely patterned walling."},"Heliopolis":{"era":"Old Kingdom onward","where":"Egypt","text":"Heliopolis, near modern Cairo, was one of Egypt's oldest religious centers and the main seat of the sun god Ra. Its priests were renowned for learning, and its obelisks were later carried to Rome and beyond."},"Caral":{"era":"Norte Chico, c. 2600–2000 BC","where":"Peru","text":"Caral in Peru's Supe Valley was a major center of the Norte Chico tradition and among the oldest cities in the Americas. Its people built large platform mounds and sunken circular plazas."},"Ife":{"era":"Yoruba, c. 11th–15th c.","where":"Nigeria","text":"Ile-Ife in southwestern Nigeria is regarded by the Yoruba as the place where the world was created. Its artists made naturalistic heads in copper alloy and terracotta of remarkable skill."},"Zeila":{"era":"Medieval port","where":"Somaliland","text":"Zeila on the Gulf of Aden was an old port linking the Horn of Africa with Arabia. In medieval times it was a major outlet for the trade of the Adal Sultanate."},"Monte Albán":{"era":"Zapotec, c. 500 BC–AD 800","where":"Mexico","text":"The Zapotec built Monte Albán on a leveled mountaintop above the Valley of Oaxaca from c. 500 BC. For over a thousand years it was their political and religious capital, with plazas, temples and carved stones."},"Lamu":{"era":"Swahili, from 14th c.","where":"Kenya","text":"Lamu is among the oldest continuously inhabited Swahili towns. Its narrow streets, coral-stone houses and carved wooden doors preserve a centuries-old coastal culture."},"Sijilmasa":{"era":"Medieval, 8th–14th c.","where":"Morocco","text":"Founded in the Tafilalt oasis in the 8th century, Sijilmasa was a key northern terminus of trans-Saharan trade. Caravans carried West African gold through its markets to the Mediterranean world."},"Moundville":{"era":"Mississippian, c. 1100–1450","where":"Alabama, USA","text":"Moundville on the Black Warrior River was a major Mississippian center c. 1100–1450. Its people arranged over two dozen earthen mounds around a large central plaza."},"Tula":{"era":"Toltec, c. 900–1150","where":"Mexico","text":"Tula in Hidalgo was the capital of the Toltecs, flourishing c. 900–1150. Its stone warrior columns stand atop a pyramid, and later peoples such as the Mexica honored the Toltecs as masters of the arts."},"Benin City":{"era":"Edo kingdom, 13th–16th c.","where":"Nigeria","text":"Benin City was the capital of the Edo kingdom of Benin, ruled by the Oba. Its royal guilds cast famous brass plaques and heads, and vast earthworks surrounded the city and its lands."},"Chavín de Huántar":{"era":"First millennium BC","where":"Peru","text":"Chavín de Huántar in the Peruvian highlands was a ceremonial center of the first millennium BC. Its stone temple, with inner galleries and carved figures, drew pilgrims and spread a shared artistic style across the Andes."},"Old Dongola":{"era":"Makurian, c. 6th–14th c.","where":"Sudan","text":"Old Dongola on the Nile was the capital of Makuria, a Christian Nubian kingdom. Its churches and palace reflect a state that kept its independence for centuries under the Baqt treaty with Egypt."},"Aswan":{"era":"Pharaonic–Roman","where":"Egypt","text":"Aswan marked Egypt's southern frontier at the First Cataract of the Nile. Its granite quarries supplied obelisks and statues, and the island of Elephantine served as a trading post with Nubia."},"La Venta":{"era":"Olmec, c. 900–400 BC","where":"Mexico","text":"La Venta in Tabasco was a major Olmec center, flourishing c. 900–400 BC. Its people built an early earthen pyramid and carved colossal stone heads and fine jade offerings."},"Tlemcen":{"era":"Zayyanid, 13th–16th c.","where":"Algeria","text":"Tlemcen in western Algeria was capital of the Zayyanid kingdom from the 13th to the 16th century. It was a center of trade, scholarship and fine Islamic architecture, including its Great Mosque."},"Mogadishu":{"era":"Medieval, from 10th c.","where":"Somalia","text":"Mogadishu grew into a wealthy Somali trading city on the Indian Ocean in medieval times. Its merchants exported fine woven cloth, and Ibn Battuta described the city during his visit in 1331."},"Mbanza Kongo":{"era":"Kingdom of Kongo, 14th–17th c.","where":"Angola","text":"Mbanza Kongo in northern Angola was the royal capital of the Kingdom of Kongo, a major state of Central Africa. From the late 15th century its rulers adopted Christianity and exchanged embassies with Portugal."},"Sofala":{"era":"Swahili port, medieval","where":"Mozambique","text":"Sofala on the coast of Mozambique was the southern port of the Swahili trading world. Gold from the Zimbabwe plateau was brought there and shipped north to Kilwa and beyond."},"Agadez":{"era":"Aïr Sultanate, from 15th c.","where":"Niger","text":"Agadez in the Aïr region was the seat of a Tuareg sultanate and a key stop on trans-Saharan caravan routes. Its mud-brick mosque minaret, first raised in the early 16th century, still dominates the town."},"Tzintzuntzan":{"era":"Purépecha, 15th–16th c.","where":"Mexico","text":"Tzintzuntzan on Lake Pátzcuaro was the capital of the Purépecha state in western Mexico. Its people built round-fronted platform temples called yácatas and were skilled metalworkers."},"Wari":{"era":"c. 600–1000","where":"Peru","text":"Wari near Ayacucho was the capital of an Andean state c. 600–1000. Its people built planned administrative centers and roads that later influenced the Inca."},"Caguana":{"era":"Taíno, c. 1200–1500","where":"Puerto Rico","text":"Caguana in the mountains of Puerto Rico was a ceremonial center of the Taíno people. Its stone-lined plazas, used for ball games and gatherings, are edged with carved petroglyphs."},"Uruk":{"era":"Sumerian, 4th–3rd millennium BC","where":"Iraq","text":"Uruk in southern Iraq was one of the first true cities, growing large in the 4th millennium BC. Early writing developed there, and it was later remembered as the city of the hero-king Gilgamesh."},"Byblos":{"era":"Bronze Age–Phoenician","where":"Lebanon","text":"Byblos on the Lebanese coast is one of the oldest continuously inhabited towns in the world. Its merchants shipped cedar to Egypt, and the Greek words for papyrus and book derive from its name."},"Harappa":{"era":"Indus Valley, c. 2600–1900 BC","where":"Pakistan","text":"Harappa in Punjab was one of the great cities of the Indus Valley Civilization. Its people built planned streets, brick houses, covered drains and large storehouses, and used a script still undeciphered."},"Bukhara":{"era":"Samanid–Shaybanid","where":"Uzbekistan","text":"Bukhara was a major Silk Road city and capital of the Samanid dynasty. It became a great center of Islamic learning, and its Kalyan Minaret has stood since the 12th century."},"Tayma":{"era":"oasis town, ancient","where":"Saudi Arabia","text":"Tayma was a desert oasis on the incense route of northwest Arabia. The Babylonian king Nabonidus lived there for about ten years in the 6th century BC."},"Chang'an":{"era":"Han–Tang","where":"Shaanxi, China","text":"Chang'an, near modern Xi'an, was the capital of the Han and Tang dynasties. Under the Tang it was one of the largest cities in the world and the eastern end of the Silk Road."},"Pasargadae":{"era":"Achaemenid, 6th c. BC","where":"Iran","text":"Cyrus the Great built Pasargadae in southwestern Iran as the first capital of the Achaemenid Persian Empire. His simple stone tomb still stands among the remains of its palaces and gardens."},"Angkor":{"era":"Khmer, 9th–15th c.","where":"Cambodia","text":"Angkor was the capital of the Khmer Empire from the 9th to the 15th century. Its builders created vast reservoirs and temples, including Angkor Wat, the largest religious monument in the world."},"Pataliputra":{"era":"Maurya–Gupta","where":"Bihar, India","text":"Pataliputra, near modern Patna, was the capital of the Maurya Empire. Under Chandragupta and Ashoka it was one of the largest cities in the world, and it later served the Gupta kings."},"Gyeongju":{"era":"Silla, 57 BC–AD 935","where":"South Korea","text":"Gyeongju was the capital of the Silla kingdom for nearly a thousand years. Its royal tombs, the Cheomseongdae observatory and nearby Bulguksa Temple reflect a rich Buddhist culture."},"Merv":{"era":"Achaemenid–Seljuk","where":"Turkmenistan","text":"Merv was a great oasis city on the Silk Road for well over a thousand years. Under the Seljuks in the 11th–12th centuries it was among the largest cities in the world."},"Eridu":{"era":"Sumerian","where":"Iraq","text":"Eridu in southern Iraq was regarded by the Sumerians as the first city, where kingship began. Its temple to the god Enki was rebuilt many times over thousands of years."},"Luoyang":{"era":"Zhou–Tang","where":"Henan, China","text":"Luoyang on the Yellow River plain served as capital for many Chinese dynasties. Its White Horse Temple is traditionally regarded as China's first Buddhist temple, and the Longmen Grottoes lie nearby."},"Ugarit":{"era":"Late Bronze Age","where":"Syria","text":"Ugarit on the Syrian coast was a wealthy trading kingdom in the Late Bronze Age. Its scribes used one of the earliest known alphabets, written in cuneiform signs on clay tablets."},"Bagan":{"era":"Pagan kingdom, 11th–13th c.","where":"Myanmar","text":"Bagan on the Irrawaddy River was the capital of the Pagan kingdom from the 11th to the 13th century. Its rulers and people built thousands of Buddhist temples and stupas across the plain."},"Qaryat al-Faw":{"era":"c. 1st c. BC – 4th c. AD","where":"Saudi Arabia","text":"Qaryat al-Faw was a caravan town on the edge of the Empty Quarter and a capital of the kingdom of Kinda. Its ruins hold markets, temples and painted walls."},"Ecbatana":{"era":"Median–Achaemenid","where":"Iran","text":"Ecbatana, modern Hamadan in western Iran, was the capital of the Medes. The Achaemenid kings later used it as a summer residence and royal treasury."},"Varanasi":{"era":"Ancient onward","where":"Uttar Pradesh, India","text":"Varanasi on the Ganges is one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in the world and a holy city of Hinduism. Nearby Sarnath is where the Buddha gave his first sermon."},"Khiva":{"era":"Khwarezm, medieval–16th c.","where":"Uzbekistan","text":"Khiva was an oasis town of Khwarezm on the edge of the desert. Its walled inner city, Itchan Kala, preserves mosques, madrasas and palaces within high mud-brick walls."},"Kaifeng":{"era":"Northern Song, 960–1127","where":"Henan, China","text":"Kaifeng was the capital of the Northern Song dynasty. It was a bustling center of commerce and culture, widely thought to be the city shown in the scroll Along the River During the Qingming Festival."},"Nippur":{"era":"Sumerian–Babylonian","where":"Iraq","text":"Nippur in central Iraq was the religious heart of Sumer, home of the temple of the god Enlil. Kings sought recognition there, and its scribal schools preserved much Sumerian literature."},"Ayutthaya":{"era":"1351–1767","where":"Thailand","text":"Founded in 1351, Ayutthaya was the capital of a Siamese kingdom for over four centuries. Its island setting where three rivers meet made it a great trading port for merchants from Asia and Europe."},"Taxila":{"era":"Gandhara, c. 6th c. BC–5th c. AD","where":"Pakistan","text":"Taxila was a crossroads city of Gandhara for many centuries. It was famed as a center of learning and Buddhist art, blending Indian, Persian and Greek influences."},"Kaesong":{"era":"Goryeo, 918–1392","where":"Korea","text":"Kaesong was the capital of the Goryeo dynasty of Korea from the 10th to the 14th century. Goryeo was known for its celadon pottery, and the English name Korea comes from it."},"Jericho":{"era":"Neolithic onward","where":"Jordan Valley","text":"Jericho in the Jordan Valley is one of the oldest inhabited sites in the world. Around 8000 BC its people built a stone wall and tower, among the earliest known."},"Marib":{"era":"Sabaean, 1st millennium BC","where":"Yemen","text":"Marib was the capital of the kingdom of Saba, which grew rich on the incense trade. Its great dam watered the surrounding oasis for over a thousand years."},"Isfahan":{"era":"Seljuk–Safavid","where":"Iran","text":"Isfahan in central Iran was an old trading city that became the Safavid capital in 1598. Shah Abbas I rebuilt it around a vast square lined with mosques, a palace and a grand bazaar."},"Balkh":{"era":"Bactrian–Islamic","where":"Afghanistan","text":"Balkh, called Bactra by the Greeks, was one of the oldest cities of Central Asia. It was a capital of Bactria and an important center of Buddhism and later of Islamic learning."},"Ujjain":{"era":"Ancient–medieval","where":"Madhya Pradesh, India","text":"Ujjain was the capital of the ancient kingdom of Avanti and is one of Hinduism's seven sacred cities. It was a center of astronomy, and Indian astronomers long used it as their prime meridian."},"Hangzhou":{"era":"Southern Song, 12th–13th c.","where":"Zhejiang, China","text":"Hangzhou became the capital of the Southern Song dynasty in the 12th century. Set beside the scenic West Lake, it was one of the richest and most populous cities of its age."},"Sukhothai":{"era":"13th–14th c.","where":"Thailand","text":"Sukhothai in north-central Thailand was the capital of an early Thai kingdom in the 13th–14th centuries. Its graceful Buddhist art and early Thai writing are closely associated with this period."},"Ashur":{"era":"Assyrian","where":"Iraq","text":"Ashur on the Tigris was the first capital and religious center of Assyria, sharing its name with the chief god. From here Assyrian merchants ran a long-distance trade network reaching into Anatolia."},"Ebla":{"era":"3rd millennium BC","where":"Syria","text":"Ebla in northern Syria was a powerful kingdom in the third millennium BC. Excavations found a palace archive of thousands of clay tablets recording its trade and government."},"Vijayanagara":{"era":"1336–1565","where":"Karnataka, India","text":"Vijayanagara, at Hampi, was the capital of a powerful South Indian empire founded in 1336. Its temples, markets and waterworks made it one of the largest cities of its time until it was abandoned after 1565."},"Herat":{"era":"Timurid, 15th c.","where":"Afghanistan","text":"Herat was an ancient oasis city on the routes between Persia and Central Asia. Under the Timurids in the 15th century it flourished as a center of painting, poetry and architecture."},"Nara":{"era":"Nara period, 710–784","where":"Japan","text":"Nara became Japan's first permanent capital in 710. Its great Buddhist temples, including Todai-ji with its giant bronze Buddha, made it the heart of the early Japanese state and religion."},"Shibam":{"era":"Hadramawt, medieval","where":"Yemen","text":"Shibam in the Hadramawt valley was a walled trading town on the incense routes. Its tightly packed mud-brick tower houses, many several stories high, make it one of the oldest high-rise towns in the world."},"Nanjing":{"era":"Six Dynasties–Ming","where":"Jiangsu, China","text":"Nanjing on the Yangtze served as capital for several Chinese dynasties. The first Ming emperor made it his capital and enclosed it with one of the longest city walls ever built."},"Tabriz":{"era":"Ilkhanid–Safavid","where":"Iran","text":"Tabriz in northwestern Iran was a major trading city on routes linking Asia and Europe. It served as capital of the Ilkhanate and the early Safavids, and its covered bazaar is among the world's largest."},"Malacca":{"era":"Sultanate, c. 1400–1511","where":"Malaysia","text":"Malacca was founded c. 1400 and became a powerful sultanate controlling the Strait of Malacca. Traders from China, India, Arabia and the islands met in its busy port."},"Nimrud":{"era":"Neo-Assyrian, 9th–7th c. BC","where":"Iraq","text":"Nimrud, ancient Kalhu, was made the Assyrian capital by Ashurnasirpal II in the 9th century BC. Its palaces held carved stone reliefs, winged guardian figures and fine ivories."},"Madurai":{"era":"Pandya, ancient–medieval","where":"Tamil Nadu, India","text":"Madurai was the capital of the Pandya kings and a center of early Tamil literature. Its great Meenakshi Temple, with towering gateways, has long made it a place of pilgrimage."},"Otrar":{"era":"Medieval Silk Road","where":"Kazakhstan","text":"Otrar on the Syr Darya was an important oasis town on the Silk Road. It was a major caravan and trading center, and the conqueror Timur died there in 1405."},"Anyang":{"era":"Shang, c. 1300–1046 BC","where":"Henan, China","text":"Anyang was the site of Yin, the last capital of the Shang dynasty. Oracle bones found there carry the earliest known Chinese writing."},"Aleppo":{"era":"Ancient–Ottoman","where":"Syria","text":"Aleppo in northern Syria is one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities. Its hilltop citadel and covered markets made it a great hub of the caravan trade between the Mediterranean and the East."},"Thang Long":{"era":"Ly–Le, from 1010","where":"Vietnam","text":"Thang Long, on the site of modern Hanoi, became the capital of Vietnam's Ly dynasty in 1010. It remained the center of royal government for centuries, with its citadel and Temple of Literature."},"Sana'a":{"era":"Ancient–medieval","where":"Yemen","text":"Sana'a in the highlands of Yemen has been inhabited for over 2,500 years. Its old city is known for tall tower houses decorated with white geometric patterns and for its early Great Mosque."},"Fatehpur Sikri":{"era":"Mughal, 1570s–1580s","where":"Uttar Pradesh, India","text":"The Mughal emperor Akbar built Fatehpur Sikri near Agra as his capital in the 1570s. Its red sandstone palaces, mosque and Buland Darwaza gateway blend Persian and Indian styles."},"Shiraz":{"era":"Medieval","where":"Iran","text":"Shiraz in southern Iran became a center of Persian culture, gardens and poetry. The great poets Saadi and Hafez lived there, and their tombs remain places of visit and remembrance."},"Mari":{"era":"Bronze Age","where":"Syria","text":"Mari on the Euphrates was a rich trading kingdom of the Bronze Age. Its royal palace held an archive of thousands of tablets that reveal diplomacy, trade and daily life."},"Kyoto":{"era":"Heian onward, from 794","where":"Japan","text":"Kyoto, founded as Heian-kyo in 794, was the seat of Japan's imperial court for over a thousand years. It became the center of classical Japanese literature, arts and Buddhist temples."},"Kashgar":{"era":"Silk Road","where":"Xinjiang, China","text":"Kashgar in the Tarim Basin was a key oasis where Silk Road routes met. For centuries merchants from China, Central Asia and India traded in its markets."},"Dunhuang":{"era":"Han–Yuan","where":"Gansu, China","text":"Dunhuang was an oasis town on the Silk Road at the western edge of China. Nearby, the Mogao Caves hold Buddhist paintings and sculptures made over about a thousand years."},"Trowulan":{"era":"Majapahit, 13th–15th c.","where":"Indonesia","text":"Trowulan in East Java is believed to be the site of the Majapahit capital. Majapahit was a powerful maritime state from the 13th to the 15th century, and the site holds brick temples and gateways."},"Anuradhapura":{"era":"c. 4th c. BC–11th c. AD","where":"Sri Lanka","text":"Anuradhapura was the capital of Sri Lanka for over a thousand years and a center of Theravada Buddhism. Its great stupas surround a sacred fig tree said to grow from a cutting of the Bodhi tree."},"Baalbek":{"era":"Roman, 1st–3rd c.","where":"Lebanon","text":"Baalbek in Lebanon's Beqaa Valley held one of the largest temple complexes of the Roman world. Its temples of Jupiter and Bacchus were built with enormous stone blocks."},"Baghdad":{"era":"Abbasid, from 762","where":"Iraq","text":"The Abbasid caliph al-Mansur founded Baghdad in 762 as a round city on the Tigris. It became one of the largest cities in the world and a great center of learning, home to the House of Wisdom."},"Hegra":{"era":"Nabataean, 1st c. BC–1st c. AD","where":"Saudi Arabia","text":"Hegra in northwestern Arabia was the second city of the Nabataean kingdom after Petra. Its people carved more than a hundred monumental tombs into the sandstone outcrops of the desert."},"Nishapur":{"era":"Sasanian–Seljuk","where":"Iran","text":"Nishapur in northeastern Iran was a leading city of Khorasan on the Silk Road. It was known for its pottery and scholars, and the poet and mathematician Omar Khayyam was born there."},"Karakorum":{"era":"Mongol Empire, 13th c.","where":"Mongolia","text":"Karakorum in central Mongolia was the capital of the Mongol Empire in the 13th century. Developed under Ögedei Khan, it drew envoys, merchants and craftsmen from across Asia and Europe."},"Polonnaruwa":{"era":"11th–13th c.","where":"Sri Lanka","text":"Polonnaruwa became Sri Lanka's capital after Anuradhapura in the 11th century. King Parakramabahu I enriched it with palaces, temples, great rock-cut Buddha statues and a vast reservoir."},"Khanbaliq":{"era":"Yuan, 13th–14th c.","where":"Beijing, China","text":"Khanbaliq, on the site of modern Beijing, was the capital of the Yuan dynasty, built under Kublai Khan. Marco Polo described its splendor, and the Ming later rebuilt it as Beijing."},"Kamakura":{"era":"Kamakura period, 1185–1333","where":"Japan","text":"Kamakura was the seat of Japan's first shogunate, founded by Minamoto no Yoritomo in the late 12th century. Its Great Buddha, a large bronze statue, and its Zen temples recall that era."},"Palembang":{"era":"Srivijaya, 7th–11th c.","where":"Indonesia","text":"Palembang on Sumatra was the center of Srivijaya, a Buddhist maritime power that controlled key sea lanes. Chinese pilgrims studied Buddhism there on their way to India."}}};
